xnu: the boot no longer waits 62 s for a daemon and a clock that do not exist

Three timeouts ran in full on every boot of the M1 (boots ht01, df01, tb11, ds06, ds07:
root mounted 60.4 s after the kernel banner, shell prompt 66.4 s):

  30 s  IOKitInitializeTime() waits for an IORTC resource; this machine has no RTC driver.
  27 s  the waitQuiet(30 s) on the registry root that SiliconForest added after it. It could
        never succeed: StartIOKitMatching() adds a busy count to the root "until the IOKit
        daemon has completely finished launching", released only by
        IOService::iokitDaemonLaunched() on kernelmanagerd's Daemon Ready request, which
        nothing in SiliconForest sends.
   5 s  launchctl's IOKitWaitQuiet(5 s) before the single-user shell (launchctl.c
        do_single_user_mode2; a multi-user boot has the same wait after loading the
        LaunchDaemons), for the same reason.

While the daemon's count is outstanding, waitQuiet() on the root now returns when that count
is the only one left, that is when every driver has finished matching and starting. The
count itself is not released, so what a really quiet root triggers (publishHiddenMedia,
OSKext::considerUnloads) stays exactly as before: it never ran on this system and still
does not. IOKitInitializeTime() waits for the registry first and skips the clock wait once
it is quiet (a driver with a clock publishes IORTC while it starts); if the registry does
not settle in time it names the busy services and falls back to the upstream RTC wait.

Boot-args: sf_quiet_wait=<s> (registry wait, default 30, 0 none), sf_rtc_wait=<s> (RTC wait,
0 none; default 0 after a quiet registry and 30 otherwise), sf_daemon_hold_quiet=0 (the
root stays busy for waiters until the daemon reports ready, as upstream).
UPSTREAM: the IOKitInitializeTime order yes; the daemon count needs a SiliconForest decision
(a daemon that reports ready, or NO_KEXTD).

